Maintenance Schedule
A routine for keeping FredAgriMarket secure, recoverable and healthy in production. Adjust owners and times to suit your team.
Stack in scope: PHP 8.1+, MySQL 8 / MariaDB 10.4+, web server (Apache/Nginx), and front-end assets loaded from CDNs (Tailwind, Font Awesome, Google Fonts). The app has no Composer or npm dependencies.
1. Backup & Recovery
What to back up
Asset	Why	Method
Database (fredagrimarket)	Holds all users, products, orders and messages. It is the only data that cannot be recreated.	mysqldump
Application code	Restorable from Git, but keep the deployed version tagged	Git tags / releases
Server config (config.php overrides, environment variables, web server vhost, PHP settings)	Needed to rebuild quickly	Encrypted copy in a secure vault, never in Git
Schedule
Frequency	Task
Daily (off-peak, e.g. 02:00 EAT)	Full database dump, compressed and encrypted
Hourly (optional, if order volume is high)	Binary-log or incremental backup to reduce data loss
Daily	Copy the dump off the server (cloud storage or a separate host)
Before every deployment	Manual database dump and a Git tag of the current release
Monthly	Test restore into a staging database and confirm tables and row counts
Quarterly	Full disaster-recovery drill (rebuild a server from backups and docs)


Example daily backup command
mysqldump --single-transaction --routines --triggers \
  -u backup_user -p fredagrimarket | gzip > fredagrimarket_$(date +%F).sql.gz
--single-transaction takes a consistent snapshot of the InnoDB tables without locking the shop during the backup.
Retention
•	Daily backups: keep 14 days
•	Weekly backups (Sunday): keep 8 weeks
•	Monthly backups: keep 12 months
Recovery procedure
1.	Assess: identify what failed (server, database corruption, bad deployment, data deleted) and put the site in maintenance mode if needed.
2.	Restore code: check out the last known-good Git tag on the server.
3.	Restore config: reapply environment variables (DB_HOST, DB_NAME, DB_USER, DB_PASS) and web server settings.
4.	Restore data: 
5.	gunzip < fredagrimarket_YYYY-MM-DD.sql.gz | mysql -u root -p fredagrimarket
6.	Verify: check the homepage, log in, browse the catalogue, view a recent order and api/produce.php. Place a test order and confirm stock decrements.
7.	Reconcile: compare orders against any records after the backup time (payment confirmations, emails) and follow up manually.
8.	Document: record the cause, time to restore and any data lost.
Recovery targets
Target	Goal
RPO (maximum data loss)	24 hours (reduce to 1 hour with incremental backups)
RTO (maximum downtime)	4 hours






2. Dependency Updates
The app has no package manager, so "dependencies" means the runtime stack and externally hosted assets.
Component	Check frequency	Action
PHP (security patch releases)	Monthly, and immediately for critical CVEs	Apply patch updates on staging first, then production
PHP minor/major version	Every 6 months	Review supported versions; upgrade before the current version loses security support
MySQL / MariaDB	Monthly for patches; yearly for version review	Take a backup first, then upgrade staging, then production
Web server (Apache/Nginx) and OS packages	Weekly (enable automatic security updates)	Reboot in a maintenance window when kernel updates require it
Tailwind CSS (CDN script)	Quarterly	Pin to a specific version rather than the floating CDN build, and check the layout after updating. For production, consider compiling Tailwind and serving a local CSS file
Font Awesome / Google Fonts	Quarterly	Check for new versions, and add Subresource Integrity (SRI) hashes where supported
Update workflow: back up → apply on staging → run the smoke test below → deploy to production in a quiet period → monitor for 24 hours.
Smoke test: register, log in, search, add to cart, apply ORGANIC10, check out, view the receipt, then use the farmer dashboard and admin panel.





3. Security Checks
Frequency	Check
Daily	Review web server and PHP error logs for repeated failed logins, unusual 403/419/500 errors and suspicious requests
Weekly	Confirm that backups ran and are encrypted. Check for new unrecognised admin or farmer accounts
Monthly	Apply OS and PHP security patches. Review admin and farmer users, and remove inactive ones. Check that setup_admin.php is not on the server. Review active promo codes. Confirm the HTTPS certificate is valid and auto-renewing
Quarterly	Rotate the database password and any API or payment credentials. Review file permissions (config.php not world-readable, no writable code directories). Verify the database user has only the privileges it needs (not root). Run a vulnerability scan (e.g. OWASP ZAP) on staging
Every 6 months	Review the code for the OWASP Top 10: confirm prepared statements, e() output escaping and CSRF checks are used in any new code. Check security headers (CSP, X-Frame-Options, HSTS)
Annually	Full security review or external penetration test, and a review of user data handling and retention
Always:
•	Remove or change demo accounts (Farmer@123) before going live
•	Never commit credentials to Git
•	Investigate any security incident immediately, then record and fix the root cause














4. Application Health Monitoring
Continuous (automated, with alerts)
What	How	Alert when
Uptime	External monitor (e.g. UptimeRobot) pinging index.php and api/produce.php every 1–5 minutes	Down for more than 2 checks
API response	Check api/produce.php returns HTTP 200 and valid JSON with count and data	Any 4xx/5xx or invalid JSON
Response time	Track page load time	Consistently above 3 seconds
Server resources	CPU, memory, disk	CPU or memory above 85%, disk above 80%
Database	Connection count, slow queries, replication or backup status	Connection errors, or queries over 2 seconds
SSL certificate	Expiry check	Fewer than 14 days remaining
Error logs	Aggregate PHP and web server logs	Spike in 500 errors or PDOException messages
Business health (check daily, or build into the admin panel)
•	Orders stuck in pending for more than 24 hours
•	Products with zero stock or farms awaiting verification
•	Unread contact messages
•	Sudden drops in order volume (may indicate a checkout fault)
Regular reviews
Frequency	Review
Weekly	Error log trends, slow pages, uptime report
Monthly	Resource usage trends and database growth. Clean up old session files if using file-based sessions
Quarterly	Capacity planning, and database index and query review as order volume grows





